  {
    "label": "figure_title",
    "text": "FIG. 4. From circular channels to continuous particle collection. a. Circular annular channel used to generate radial demixing between fast and slow particles. b. Three-way sorting geometry obtained by combining two shifted semicircular channels and introducing a separating wall that directs the demixed populations towards distinct inner and outer outlets. The wall position is parametrized by w = (Rwall − Rin)/(Rout − Rin). c. Steady-state particle fractions χ1n and χ2n at the inner outlet as a function of w. The purity of fast particles is maximal around w ≈ 0.2. d. Corresponding particle fractions χ1ut and χout at the outer outlet, with maximal purity of slow particles around w ≈ 0.5. e. Normalized throughput of fast particles recovered at the inner outlet, Q1, and slow particles recovered at the outer outlet, Q2, as a function of w. Increasing w enhances the recovery of fast particles while reducing that of slow particles, revealing the trade-off between outlet purity and particle recovery.",
